upfront which information will be

1248
02:03:47,140 --> 02:03:50,860
valuable to which user group. Our audiences are incredibly

1249
02:03:50,920 --> 02:03:54,420
diverse. Scientists, historians, museum curators, schools,

1250
02:03:54,620 --> 02:03:58,360
artists, and local communities seeking to reconnect with objects from their

1251
02:03:58,400 --> 02:03:59,460
countries of origin.

1252
02:04:00,600 --> 02:04:04,240
This data must be standardized, but we must also consider that vastly different

1253
02:04:04,300 --> 02:04:06,320
objects coexist within our collection.

1254
02:04:06,960 --> 02:04:10,480
For example, a series of common beetles will not have the same metadata

1255
02:04:11,000 --> 02:04:14,480
as a complete skeleton of Dysalotosaurus or as Jacob, the

1256
02:04:14,780 --> 02:04:16,740
Alexander von Humboldt beloved pet.

1257
02:04:19,340 --> 02:04:22,500
Specify is invaluable for two key reasons.

1258
02:04:22,940 --> 02:04:25,900
First, its interface, it's easy for end users to adopt,

1259
02:04:26,780 --> 02:04:30,660
and second, and it is what truly sets Specify apart, it is

1260
02:04:30,720 --> 02:04:34,719
flexibility. Unlike rigid relational databases, Specify

1261
02:04:34,800 --> 02:04:38,740
custom table fields and interconnecting links adapt to any

1262
02:04:38,800 --> 02:04:39,360
collection.

1263
02:04:40,440 --> 02:04:44,260
This flexibility has allowed us to consolidate all 30 collections

1264
02:04:44,300 --> 02:04:47,520
into a single Specify instance, meaning just one database.

1265
02:04:48,400 --> 02:04:51,800
It facilitates standardization and homogenization of data while

1266
02:04:51,920 --> 02:04:55,700
preserving the unique characteristics of each collection.

1267
02:04:56,760 --> 02:05:00,240
For collection staff, this unified structure fosters better understanding

1268
02:05:00,660 --> 02:05:04,360
and collaboration. By grouping similar concepts,

1269
02:05:04,720 --> 02:05:08,460
they recognize the value of standardization and see that while each

1270
02:05:08,500 --> 02:05:12,440
collection has its unique aspects, they all share

1271
02:05:12,480 --> 02:05:13,240
commonalities.

1272
02:05:14,060 --> 02:05:17,420
This encourages a more cohesive and inclusive approach.

1273
02:05:18,700 --> 02:05:22,520
For data managers, this means we can generate annual and monthly

1274
02:05:22,560 --> 02:05:25,780
reports, statistics, and graphs for all collections within a single query.

1275
02:05:26,300 --> 02:05:29,920
This saves time and effort while enabling efficient monitoring of

1276
02:05:29,980 --> 02:05:32,200
processes across all collections at a glance.

1277
02:05:32,540 --> 02:05:36,500
For example, here we have the number of entries created per year since the

1278
02:05:36,520 --> 02:05:37,640
database was established.

1279
02:05:39,020 --> 02:05:42,340
And here we have the percentage of NOID assigned to objects per collection,

1280
02:05:42,800 --> 02:05:46,600
allowing us to easily track the progress of this long and laborious process.

1281
02:05:48,220 --> 02:05:52,160
For developers, this centralized structure simplifies data sharing on

1282
02:05:52,200 --> 02:05:55,960
the data portal, making it easier to integrate and

1283
02:05:56,000 --> 02:05:58,080
display information consistently.

1284
02:05:59,700 --> 02:06:03,580
Specify's flexibility has been instrumental in the decolonization process at MfN,

1285
02:06:04,120 --> 02:06:07,960
allowing us to explore new concepts and immediately put them into practice.

1286
02:06:09,460 --> 02:06:13,380
To achieve this, I use a one-to-many table linking collection objects to any number

1287
02:06:13,420 --> 02:06:16,520
of historical events. These events can range from

1288
02:06:17,140 --> 02:06:20,640
before to after acquisition, from major historical

1289
02:06:20,760 --> 02:06:23,460
occurrences like those tied to war or colonialism,

1290
02:06:24,320 --> 02:06:24,900
to recent

1291
02:06:26,320 --> 02:06:28,540
localized events that we still want to document.

1292
02:06:29,900 --> 02:06:32,940
I also set up a prototype collection called Historical

1293
02:06:33,040 --> 02:06:36,960
Context. In this exploratory collection, we can link

1294
02:06:36,980 --> 02:06:40,699
transcription data, keywords, and taxa to a letter written in

1295
02:06:40,740 --> 02:06:44,300
1909 in Tanga, Uro. But most importantly,

1296
02:06:44,699 --> 02:06:48,220
it is possible to connect it to the various objects it mentions.

1297
02:06:48,860 --> 02:06:52,400
And of course, from any biological object, we can track back to all

1298
02:06:52,460 --> 02:06:55,600
archives that reference it.

1299
02:06:57,080 --> 02:07:00,280
Even though Specify offers many possibilities, gaps remain.

1300
02:07:00,400 --> 02:07:03,240
For example, in managing and standardizing historical events.

1301
02:07:03,940 --> 02:07:07,780
The main challenge is that historical events were not designed for a dedicated

1302
02:07:07,940 --> 02:07:10,440
table. I had to repurpose another table.

1303
02:07:10,920 --> 02:07:14,420
This solution isn't perfect for several reasons, but it's a work in

1304
02:07:14,480 --> 02:07:18,440
progress, and I'm confident this gap will improve

1305
02:07:18,580 --> 02:07:21,820
over time through collaboration with the Specify communities.

1306
02:07:23,540 --> 02:07:26,420
Another gap in Specify is managing localities and geography.

1307
02:07:26,940 --> 02:07:30,600
After months of working across collections, I realized

1308
02:07:30,800 --> 02:07:33,900
how difficult it is to consistently standardize

1309
02:07:34,040 --> 02:07:35,360
geographical data.

1310
02:07:36,560 --> 02:07:39,900
Political boundaries, Specify default, have advantages.

1311
02:07:40,180 --> 02:07:44,020
ISO 3166 codes ensure global standardization and

1312
02:07:44,080 --> 02:07:47,740
interoperability, help document geographic origin

1313
02:07:47,920 --> 02:07:51,700
even for historical localities like German colonies, and support

1314
02:07:51,860 --> 02:07:53,060
multi-language display.

1315
02:07:53,980 --> 02:07:57,680
However, political boundaries may not correspond to biological

1316
02:07:57,760 --> 02:07:58,480
collection needs.

1317
02:07:59,440 --> 02:08:01,480
There are anomalies in geographical locations,

1318
02:08:01,840 --> 02:08:05,460
ambiguous territories, and inconsistent

1319
02:08:05,520 --> 02:08:07,100
geographic scales,

1320
02:08:08,000 --> 02:08:10,400
which make global standardization difficult.

1321
02:08:11,740 --> 02:08:15,380
A major limitation is that administrative boundaries rarely

1322
02:08:15,520 --> 02:08:19,080
align with ecological or biogeographic zones.

1323
02:08:19,960 --> 02:08:23,760
And a species distribution can span multiple regions,

1324
02:08:24,380 --> 02:08:28,180
and these boundaries struggle to capture natural features like ocean-montane

1325
02:08:28,340 --> 02:08:31,900
ranges or ecological regions, all vital for

1326
02:08:32,000 --> 02:08:35,712
biodiversity research. Assigning a locality solely to

1327
02:08:36,112 --> 02:08:39,612
an administrative entity risks losing critical ecological context,

1328
02:08:40,032 --> 02:08:43,152
especially for specimens from remote or border areas.

1329
02:08:44,532 --> 02:08:46,372
Ecological dimensions are

1330
02:08:48,472 --> 02:08:52,452
as essential as administrative ones, capturing both

1331
02:08:52,472 --> 02:08:56,292
the geographical and political history of specimens and their

1332
02:08:56,332 --> 02:08:59,832
natural distribution. However, it is difficult to have both

1333
02:08:59,872 --> 02:09:03,412
representations simultaneously, making cross-collection

1334
02:09:03,512 --> 02:09:07,492
searches harder, especially when specimens have both administrative

1335
02:09:07,552 --> 02:09:08,332
and marine data.

1336
02:09:09,652 --> 02:09:13,592
One possible solution is to add multiple fields to the locality detail table

1337
02:09:13,652 --> 02:09:17,492
for different geographic units, like maritime areas,

1338
02:09:17,572 --> 02:09:19,012
natural features, or water bodies.

1339
02:09:19,532 --> 02:09:23,392
However, this has significantly increased the complexity and the risk of

1340
02:09:23,452 --> 02:09:24,832
error in the locality table.

1341
02:09:25,752 --> 02:09:29,252
Another idea would be to add a second geographic tree based on natural units.

1342
02:09:29,892 --> 02:09:32,992
Existing standards like geoname or MarineRegion could be used,

1343
02:09:33,852 --> 02:09:34,112
though

1344
02:09:35,052 --> 02:09:38,912
none are universally adopted. But all this still

1345
02:09:38,952 --> 02:09:39,772
needs to be tested.

1346
02:09:41,932 --> 02:09:45,752
In conclusion, while there is strong desire to share data, the

1347
02:09:45,792 --> 02:09:48,912
reality is that it comes with its own set of challenges.

1348
02:09:49,552 --> 02:09:53,052
These collections are not static. They are living, evolving entities.

1349
02:09:53,532 --> 02:09:57,292
Every day, loan requests are made for new research, and every day new

1350
02:09:57,352 --> 02:10:00,112
data must be integrated into what already exists.

1351
02:10:01,172 --> 02:10:04,532
This constant evolution adds layers of complexity to our work.

1352
02:10:05,832 --> 02:10:09,692
The concepts preserve, standardize, and reconstruct can seem antagonistic,

1353
02:10:10,112 --> 02:10:13,912
because how can we standardize something that is still in the process of being

1354
02:10:13,972 --> 02:10:17,912
constructed? This is why standardization must remain a response to a

1355
02:10:17,972 --> 02:10:20,572
need rather than a solution to a problem.

1356
02:10:21,112 --> 02:10:24,412
Even if the data appear chaotic, it reflects a complex history.

1357
02:10:25,632 --> 02:10:29,592
Despite these challenges, Specify helps us preserve the richness of

1358
02:10:29,652 --> 02:10:33,252
our collections, standardize data for consistency, and

1359
02:10:33,332 --> 02:10:35,492
reconstruct the history of specimens and the

1360
02:10:36,572 --> 02:10:37,932
knowledge they hold.

1361
02:10:39,492 --> 02:10:43,412
Of course, challenges remain, whether they are geographical, historical, or

1362
02:10:43,512 --> 02:10:47,472
ethical, but this work is in progress, and researchers, curators, data

1363
02:10:47,532 --> 02:10:51,012
managers, and institutions around the world play a crucial

1364
02:10:51,112 --> 02:10:53,952
role in overcoming these obstacles.

1365
02:10:56,472 --> 02:10:57,532
So the journey continues,

1366
02:10:59,272 --> 02:11:02,212
and I'm glad that we can do that together. Thank you.

