1487
02:18:34,129 --> 02:18:37,528
But what we want to do is give everybody a big head start so that they can take the

1488
02:18:37,568 --> 02:18:41,148
data that's in their customized database and immediately share it with data

1489
02:18:41,268 --> 02:18:41,848
aggregators.

1490
02:18:44,748 --> 02:18:48,629
So we've built a new mapping tool that is something that is intuitive enough

1491
02:18:48,688 --> 02:18:52,627
for a collection manager to be able to see and understand, which

1492
02:18:52,688 --> 02:18:56,408
previously tools have been quite difficult to use, if anyone has tested those

1493
02:18:56,469 --> 02:19:00,228
mapping interfaces. We wanted to make sure that our tool also supported

1494
02:19:00,668 --> 02:19:04,508
not only just Occurrence Core with Darwin Core, but also Event and Taxon

1495
02:19:04,568 --> 02:19:08,549
Cores, because we know people are managing these to various levels within their

1496
02:19:08,568 --> 02:19:09,129
databases.

1497
02:19:10,348 --> 02:19:13,668
We also wanted to make sure we supported every GBIF-registered extension out of the

1498
02:19:13,709 --> 02:19:17,688
box, as well as extensions that have not yet been registered, so custom extensions

1499
02:19:17,728 --> 02:19:19,448
for other platforms are also supported.

1500
02:19:20,528 --> 02:19:23,889
And when I say custom, because you all understand Specify is deeply

1501
02:19:23,928 --> 02:19:27,049
customizable, we also made sure that you can have custom cores, terms,

1502
02:19:27,108 --> 02:19:30,809
extensions beyond just the ones that I'll be showing in this presentation.

1503
02:19:33,348 --> 02:19:36,049
So to give you an idea of what a mapping interface looks like,

1504
02:19:36,889 --> 02:19:40,028
anyone who's used the Specify Query Builder is already familiar with this sort of

1505
02:19:40,088 --> 02:19:43,648
interface, where you essentially get to pick out of a list all of the terms

1506
02:19:44,068 --> 02:19:46,288
or fields that exist within your database.

1507
02:19:46,969 --> 02:19:50,448
And on the left-hand side here, you can actually map those to terms.

1508
02:19:50,928 --> 02:19:54,168
So at the very top, you have tabs to go between your main

1509
02:19:54,908 --> 02:19:57,268
mapping as well as all of your extensions.

1510
02:19:59,288 --> 02:20:02,848
You get the full query builder, so all of the filters you do to be able to exclude

1511
02:20:02,888 --> 02:20:06,528
certain records based on your criteria or to return certain columns of

1512
02:20:06,568 --> 02:20:07,988
data are still available there.

1513
02:20:09,528 --> 02:20:11,488
You have the term selector on the left,

1514
02:20:12,328 --> 02:20:16,168
and very importantly, if you want to see what the term is defined as in

1515
02:20:16,208 --> 02:20:19,568
Darwin Core, you can see that directly from the application itself.

1516
02:20:19,688 --> 02:20:23,148
You're not jumping out and back into Specify. You're not searching for terms.

1517
02:20:23,688 --> 02:20:27,088
Very fortunately, you can click on the I or hover over it to

1518
02:20:27,188 --> 02:20:30,908
see a full textual description of what that term is in that

1519
02:20:30,968 --> 02:20:31,948
publishing vocabulary.

1520
02:20:33,808 --> 02:20:37,092
When you click the terms list, you can see that they're actually grouped By

1521
02:20:37,132 --> 02:20:40,972
category, so you have all of your organism fields together, material entity

1522
02:20:41,052 --> 02:20:44,952
fields together, and this sort of thing makes it really trivial to figure out

1523
02:20:45,012 --> 02:20:48,772
which concepts exist together when you're mapping your Specify database

1524
02:20:48,912 --> 02:20:49,732
to Darwin Core.

1525
02:20:52,552 --> 02:20:55,452
And you can see at the very top, that's where you can set any custom term you'd

1526
02:20:55,492 --> 02:20:59,412
like. So if there's an IRI for a term that exists in a newer version of Darwin

1527
02:20:59,472 --> 02:21:02,672
Core that isn't present in the version of Specify you're using, you don't become

1528
02:21:02,692 --> 02:21:06,612
dependent on waiting for an update from the Specify consortium to get those

1529
02:21:06,652 --> 02:21:07,592
terms available to you.

1530
02:21:10,372 --> 02:21:14,232
When you click on that I, you'll actually see a pop-up that shows you the term,

1531
02:21:14,412 --> 02:21:18,312
the vocabulary, the IRI, and description, and that makes it easy for you

1532
02:21:18,352 --> 02:21:21,812
to then see examples of how that term is used, what it should look like when

1533
02:21:21,852 --> 02:21:25,732
publishing. It's a very nice visual and link-out to be able to

1534
02:21:25,772 --> 02:21:26,552
see and use.

1535
02:21:29,932 --> 02:21:33,552
The mapping interface is intimidating enough because now you have to think, what

1536
02:21:33,872 --> 02:21:37,212
should I map to? What should I use? Which terms do I need to return?

1537
02:21:37,372 --> 02:21:41,172
What does GBIF expect and require when I'm publishing data? Those sorts of things.

1538
02:21:41,931 --> 02:21:42,752
But fortunately,

1539
02:21:43,672 --> 02:21:46,832
you can use an existing query that you've built in your database to be the starting

1540
02:21:46,872 --> 02:21:50,832
point. So many people are using Specify queries, exporting them, putting them

1541
02:21:50,912 --> 02:21:52,912
into the GBIF IPT, and going through those steps.

1542
02:21:53,372 --> 02:21:55,792
But what you can do instead is just start with your query,

1543
02:21:56,632 --> 02:21:56,852
and

1544
02:21:57,772 --> 02:21:58,992
sort of magically--

1545
02:21:59,832 --> 02:22:03,352
If you don't, of course, you have templates which we're providing for both the

1546
02:22:03,392 --> 02:22:07,052
primary Darwin Core and all of those extensions, so very nice that you don't have

1547
02:22:07,092 --> 02:22:08,272
to go through all that work yourself.

1548
02:22:09,392 --> 02:22:13,272
But if you don't currently have those mapped-out terms in

1549
02:22:13,292 --> 02:22:16,752
Darwin Core, it actually has a backbone that does that mapping for you,

1550
02:22:17,432 --> 02:22:18,752
and I'll talk more about that in a moment.

1551
02:22:21,032 --> 02:22:23,432
But as you can see, there's a lot of GBIF registered extensions.

1552
02:22:23,912 --> 02:22:27,812
They're all available to add, so when you've done your main mapping, you can click

1553
02:22:27,892 --> 02:22:31,492
on a plus and then add every registered

1554
02:22:31,532 --> 02:22:32,832
extension that GBIF supports.

1555
02:22:34,032 --> 02:22:37,372
So imagine things like your identification history, the GGBN

1556
02:22:37,812 --> 02:22:40,312
preparation material sample, and so forth extensions.

1557
02:22:40,792 --> 02:22:44,392
You have, of course, audio-visual core and all of the other

1558
02:22:44,432 --> 02:22:45,972
primary core extensions.

1559
02:22:47,451 --> 02:22:51,052
And this is something that also means you're able to see the descriptions,

1560
02:22:51,172 --> 02:22:53,712
names, IRIs, and so forth of all of those terms as well.

1561
02:22:53,792 --> 02:22:56,512
So those are all pulled and shown within Specify, too.

1562
02:22:58,852 --> 02:23:02,652
But to actually make this a usable system where you have these things connected

1563
02:23:02,692 --> 02:23:06,632
together, we're actually connecting the schema configuration, the database schema,

1564
02:23:06,672 --> 02:23:10,572
and so forth, with these external terms, so that you can see directly

1565
02:23:10,612 --> 02:23:14,052
when you're setting up the database and when you're customizing Specify which

1566
02:23:14,732 --> 02:23:18,672
terms exist in external vocabularies and what they are defined as, and

1567
02:23:18,712 --> 02:23:21,992
you can see that alongside your internal fields in Specify so you know what the

1568
02:23:22,032 --> 02:23:24,912
intention is of that field when setting up the database.

1569
02:23:29,832 --> 02:23:29,992
So

1570
02:23:30,932 --> 02:23:33,692
I'll go back just a couple slides to show--

1571
02:23:35,072 --> 02:23:35,852
Sorry, a few more.

1572
02:23:36,892 --> 02:23:37,772
Boop. Okay, here we go.

1573
02:23:38,732 --> 02:23:42,712
So when you add any field to your query, the first thing

1574
02:23:42,752 --> 02:23:46,552
that will happen is a suggested term will be mapped automatically.

1575
02:23:47,272 --> 02:23:50,872
So if you add the catalog number field, it will be mapped one-to-one to catalog

1576
02:23:50,912 --> 02:23:54,772
number within Darwin Core. If you add the preparations together,

1577
02:23:54,832 --> 02:23:56,332
it'll put that into preparations.

1578
02:23:56,452 --> 02:24:00,252
But if you want more depth to that, you can add extensions with material

1579
02:24:00,292 --> 02:24:03,772
entity and others, and it will build out that query to be more in-depth, and all of

1580
02:24:03,832 --> 02:24:07,712
that data will be preserved. If you choose audiovisual core as an

1581
02:24:07,752 --> 02:24:10,912
extension that you're adding to Specify's export mapping,

1582
02:24:11,392 --> 02:24:15,332
automatically all of the terms like creator, credit, license, and so forth will be

1583
02:24:15,372 --> 02:24:18,852
mapped whenever that is added. So you don't have to go through the laborious

1584
02:24:18,872 --> 02:24:22,312
process of deciding what should be included and what shouldn't be.

1585
02:24:23,672 --> 02:24:27,252
In another very powerful way, it does tell you if there are missing

1586
02:24:27,332 --> 02:24:30,972
terms that are required for publishing for a particular extension on GBIF,

1587
02:24:31,392 --> 02:24:35,232
that it makes sure to enforce the minimum field mapping that GBIF expects,

1588
02:24:35,832 --> 02:24:38,732
and it gives suggestions to make sure that you're on the right track as you're

1589
02:24:38,772 --> 02:24:39,712
working on that mapping.

1590
02:24:41,952 --> 02:24:44,232
So I don't know if you can tell, but I'm really excited.

