WildAlbum. That's the code that will take you there. That's the address.

3920
06:33:20,646 --> 06:33:24,286
And as for a couple of days ago when I was making this presentation, there were

3921
06:33:24,446 --> 06:33:27,846
25 datasets together comprising almost a million animal

3922
06:33:27,926 --> 06:33:30,026
occurrences, and this number keep growing.

3923
06:33:32,226 --> 06:33:35,346
So I would like to focus on a couple of aspects of CamtrapDP.

3924
06:33:37,066 --> 06:33:40,566
First, it is based on frictionless data framework, which means that it's easily

3925
06:33:40,826 --> 06:33:43,946
extendable. If you have some information that you want to express in your data

3926
06:33:44,006 --> 06:33:46,866
package, but there are no fields for it, you can easily add a new table.

3927
06:33:47,306 --> 06:33:50,986
All it takes is you need to provide a schema, and then if that schema is valid,

3928
06:33:51,026 --> 06:33:54,926
your data package will be valid. So that's a good thing because

3929
06:33:54,966 --> 06:33:58,166
it allows everyone to include all the information they want in a data package.

3930
06:33:58,586 --> 06:34:02,326
But it also creates a little bit of a problem because if everybody starts just

3931
06:34:02,366 --> 06:34:06,126
extending the data package the way they want to, then we lose the interoperability

3932
06:34:06,626 --> 06:34:08,046
of the entire thing.

3933
06:34:10,146 --> 06:34:13,626
Observation table already contains some fields that allow us to

3934
06:34:14,006 --> 06:34:16,986
inform about AI-generated results.

3935
06:34:17,186 --> 06:34:21,066
So there's a field called classification method, which can

3936
06:34:21,106 --> 06:34:24,986
be either human or machine, and there's a field called classified by, which

3937
06:34:25,026 --> 06:34:27,746
can contain the name of a human that classified the resource or

3938
06:34:28,906 --> 06:34:31,106
a name of the AI model that classified the resource.

3939
06:34:31,726 --> 06:34:35,646
However, in the last couple of years, the scope in which we use AI

3940
06:34:35,706 --> 06:34:37,906
in the analysis of this data has changed a lot.

3941
06:34:37,986 --> 06:34:41,706
So it's possible that those two fields are no longer enough to

3942
06:34:41,766 --> 06:34:42,126
actually

3943
06:34:43,086 --> 06:34:44,146
express this information.

3944
06:34:45,646 --> 06:34:49,606
And CamtrapDP was designed for traditional camera trap research, by which I mean

3945
06:34:49,826 --> 06:34:53,266
using store-bought camera traps to monitor medium and large mammals.

3946
06:34:53,726 --> 06:34:56,706
But there are other kinds of research as well that have similar structure that

3947
06:34:56,726 --> 06:34:58,866
could also benefit from the standard like this.

3948
06:34:59,406 --> 06:35:03,026
And I would like to tell you about a couple of projects that reevaluated CamtrapDP

3949
06:35:03,086 --> 06:35:05,786
standard for the needs of their own data.

3950
06:35:07,546 --> 06:35:09,226
First is the Safe and Sound project.

3951
06:35:09,626 --> 06:35:13,406
It was published by Wild Labs in something that is charmingly called Boring

3952
06:35:13,526 --> 06:35:17,306
Fund, and the project produced a report that you can read in full under

3953
06:35:17,346 --> 06:35:21,086
this code. And the purpose of this project was to, as I said, to

3954
06:35:21,126 --> 06:35:24,706
evaluate whether CamtrapDP is suitable for sharing

3955
06:35:25,346 --> 06:35:26,526
bioacoustic data.

3956
06:35:28,106 --> 06:35:29,686
And here are some of the conclusions.

3957
06:35:31,566 --> 06:35:35,466
The main idea is that, yes, CamtrapDP standard can be used to

3958
06:35:35,506 --> 06:35:39,406
express bioacoustic data as well because the structure of data is

3959
06:35:39,426 --> 06:35:42,706
quite similar, but there are some changes that would be required for it to be a

3960
06:35:42,766 --> 06:35:46,586
good fit for this data. So we would have to add some terms in the

3961
06:35:46,826 --> 06:35:48,986
deployments table to describe acoustic sensors.

3962
06:35:49,146 --> 06:35:53,066
Right now we have fields like camera model or camera height, so those fields

3963
06:35:53,106 --> 06:35:56,446
would have to be renamed to device model, device height, so it can accommodate

3964
06:35:56,506 --> 06:36:00,186
acoustic sensors as well as cameras. Does this work? Yes. Okay.

3965
06:36:01,426 --> 06:36:05,266
We would need to also add way to describe

3966
06:36:05,366 --> 06:36:06,926
recording schedules. There can

3967
06:36:08,106 --> 06:36:11,346
be diverse recording schedules in traditional camera trapping as well, but

3968
06:36:11,366 --> 06:36:13,086
typically it's all motion activated.

3969
06:36:13,186 --> 06:36:15,226
Something moves in front of the camera, then it takes a picture.

3970
06:36:15,686 --> 06:36:19,246
With acoustic recording, there are some more diverse recording

3971
06:36:19,326 --> 06:36:23,086
schedules. So the standard needs a way to describe that in detail.

3972
06:36:24,746 --> 06:36:28,465
There are some fields that would be required to add to the media table, because

3973
06:36:28,526 --> 06:36:32,506
right now the media table has been designed to describe, as I said, images and

3974
06:36:32,546 --> 06:36:36,506
videos. So fields like maybe frequency range or something that are typical

3975
06:36:36,586 --> 06:36:38,346
for audio data would have to be added as well.

3976
06:36:40,006 --> 06:36:43,046
And another proposition from the Safe and Sound project was that we should add

3977
06:36:43,106 --> 06:36:46,826
assertions table that would enable adding different kinds of measurements to this

3978
06:36:47,066 --> 06:36:50,346
data package. So this assertions table is an analog to

3979
06:36:52,326 --> 06:36:56,006
measurement or fact extension from Darwin Core Archive or from assertions table

3980
06:36:56,186 --> 06:36:56,386
from

3981
06:36:57,246 --> 06:37:00,866
Darwin Core data package. So that was the proposition to include it in

3982
06:37:00,926 --> 06:37:02,006
CamtrapDP as well.

3983
06:37:03,446 --> 06:37:07,426
And the other group that reevaluated CamtrapDP for their purposes

3984
06:37:07,526 --> 06:37:11,086
is InsectAI. InsectAI is a cost action. It's a big project.

3985
06:37:11,146 --> 06:37:12,646
It's over 500 participants.

3986
06:37:13,606 --> 06:37:16,226
And within this InsectAI, there is working group three.

3987
06:37:16,946 --> 06:37:20,866
And among the tasks of this group is to devise a standard for sharing data and

3988
06:37:20,926 --> 06:37:23,686
metadata from automated insect monitoring.

3989
06:37:23,826 --> 06:37:27,646
This code will take you to the website of InsectAI, so we can find out more

3990
06:37:27,706 --> 06:37:28,866
about this fantastic project.

3991
06:37:30,786 --> 06:37:34,426
And as I said, they also reevaluated the value of CamtrapDP

3992
06:37:34,606 --> 06:37:36,606
for the data for visual insect monitoring.

3993
06:37:38,326 --> 06:37:41,066
And the results were the following. It is a good fit.

3994
06:37:41,226 --> 06:37:43,326
However, some changes to the standard are necessary.

3995
06:37:43,466 --> 06:37:45,326
I hope you're starting to see a pattern here.

3996
06:37:46,946 --> 06:37:50,146
So we would need to extend some vocabularies and add some fields to better

3997
06:37:50,186 --> 06:37:53,906
accommodate insect data. For example, in deployments table, we have a

3998
06:37:53,966 --> 06:37:57,246
field called bait use because we often use some kind of bait in traditional camera

3999
06:37:57,286 --> 06:38:01,226
trapping. But in insect monitoring, it's often light or pheromones that are used

4000
06:38:01,246 --> 06:38:04,526
to attract insects. So we would have to rename this field attractant use to cover a

4001
06:38:04,566 --> 06:38:07,346
wider array of possibilities there.

4002
06:38:09,270 --> 06:38:12,870
more robust hardware description would be required to cover insect monitoring,

4003
06:38:12,970 --> 06:38:16,690
because in traditional camera trapping, you have those store-bought cameras, fairly

4004
06:38:16,730 --> 06:38:20,470
simple models. But in insect monitoring, there are often some custom-made

4005
06:38:20,550 --> 06:38:24,330
hardwares that are very interesting, so it would require a little bit more

4006
06:38:24,370 --> 06:38:25,690
fields to describe them properly.

4007
06:38:27,710 --> 06:38:31,150
A way to describe more diverse recording schedule would

4008
06:38:32,030 --> 06:38:34,750
also be helpful for the insect monitoring community.

4009
06:38:36,490 --> 06:38:37,030
And another

4010
06:38:38,010 --> 06:38:41,490
suggestion was that we should be able to express all classifications that are made

4011
06:38:41,529 --> 06:38:45,170
for the resource, not just what we currently have in the standard, a table with

4012
06:38:45,210 --> 06:38:47,570
consensus for every resource and event.

4013
06:38:47,710 --> 06:38:51,010
That was part of the monitoring project.

4014
06:38:52,210 --> 06:38:53,970
And the last piece of input from this...

4015
06:38:54,910 --> 06:38:58,190
That's a summary. So the last thing I wanted to tell you about is that the

4016
06:38:58,230 --> 06:39:02,190
suggestion was to add a table for models, because currently there's

4017
06:39:02,250 --> 06:39:05,970
only a way to have a

4018
06:39:06,030 --> 06:39:09,890
name of the model, and with the amount of models we use these

4019
06:39:09,910 --> 06:39:13,530
days, it's just not enough. So there was a suggestion to add a models table that

4020
06:39:13,530 --> 06:39:16,850
would enable us to describe used AI models in more detail.

4021
06:39:18,390 --> 06:39:22,310
Okay. And the traditional camera trapping community has also been giving some

4022
06:39:22,350 --> 06:39:25,450
feedback to the standard. For a couple of years, they have been using it now, and

4023
06:39:25,490 --> 06:39:28,970
this mostly comes from my experience of developing Trapper and talking to Trapper

4024
06:39:29,050 --> 06:39:32,870
users. So there are two points that this feedback converges onto.

4025
06:39:33,390 --> 06:39:37,270
First is that there is a need to share all identifications made for a resource,

4026
06:39:37,410 --> 06:39:41,230
like I mentioned before. When we have a single picture or video, we don't

4027
06:39:41,790 --> 06:39:45,090
run just one model on it. There is often a pipeline of different AI models that

4028
06:39:45,170 --> 06:39:47,830
look at the same resource and come to similar or different conclusions.

4029
06:39:48,510 --> 06:39:52,310
A way is needed to express all of those observations, all of those results,

4030
06:39:52,450 --> 06:39:53,990
not just the final consensus one.

4031
06:39:55,810 --> 06:39:59,690
And also more detail for describing the models, more than just a name is

4032
06:39:59,730 --> 06:40:00,270
necessary.

4033
06:40:02,970 --> 06:40:04,070
All right. So,

4034
06:40:04,990 --> 06:40:05,610
to sum this up,

4035
06:40:06,470 --> 06:40:08,810
CamtrapDB has been adopted by the community.

4036
06:40:08,930 --> 06:40:12,870
It has proven to be a good way of sharing camera trap data in a way that

4037
06:40:12,890 --> 06:40:16,450
is according to FAIR principles. There's a growing number of tools to

4038
06:40:16,470 --> 06:40:20,069
visualize this data, to analyze it, to share and publish this data so other

4039
06:40:20,090 --> 06:40:24,010
communities, not only traditional camera trapping community, could benefit from

4040
06:40:24,070 --> 06:40:26,190
a standard like this and from this whole suite of tools.

4041
06:40:27,830 --> 06:40:30,890
Feedback from different thematic communities highlights similar needs.

4042
06:40:31,010 --> 06:40:34,870
All this feedback highlights basically the changes that happened in the

4043
06:40:34,910 --> 06:40:36,970
data analysis pipelines in the last couple of years.

4044
06:40:37,350 --> 06:40:40,230
So the direction in which we need to change the standard is really

4045
06:40:41,130 --> 06:40:43,590
clear if we take all of those feedbacks into account.

4046
06:40:45,230 --> 06:40:49,130
CamtrapDB is a good fit, not just for traditional camera traps, but

4047
06:40:49,190 --> 06:40:51,590
for any data that comes from stationary sensors.

4048
06:40:51,650 --> 06:40:52,330
So it could be

4049
06:40:53,250 --> 06:40:56,690
rigs for monitoring insects or audio recorders or camera traps.

4050
06:40:56,730 --> 06:41:00,210
The structure of this data is very similar, so they can all benefit from the same

4051
06:41:00,290 --> 06:41:03,910
standard. And also, there's a lot of multimodal monitoring these days when you have

4052
06:41:03,930 --> 06:41:05,530
both camera traps and audio recorders.

4053
06:41:05,830 --> 06:41:09,650
So having this shared standard for all this data would also be beneficial to enable

4054
06:41:09,710 --> 06:41:11,750
sharing these multimodal datasets as one

4055
06:41:12,810 --> 06:41:13,230
entity.

4056
06:41:15,890 --> 06:41:19,770
So we will continue these works. The Safer and Sounder project that

4057
06:41:19,790 --> 06:41:23,450
was funded by NLBIF will start December this year, and the purpose of this

4058
06:41:23,470 --> 06:41:27,370
project is to incorporate feedback from the Safe and Sound project into the

4059
06:41:27,430 --> 06:41:29,150
standard. So that's something I'll be working on.

4060
06:41:29,570 --> 06:41:33,410
Insect AI is also still happening, so we'll be now working on

4061
06:41:33,450 --> 06:41:35,890
incorporating this feedback into the standard.

4062
06:41:37,250 --> 06:41:38,990
CamtrapDB is developed under Tadwik.

4063
06:41:39,130 --> 06:41:41,390
There's a website, and it's all happening on GitHub.

4064
06:41:41,470 --> 06:41:45,310
So if you want to stay on top of it, if you want to be a part of the discussion,

4065
06:41:45,650 --> 06:41:49,490
I encourage you all to visit our GitHub, see the issues, and take part in the

4066
06:41:49,530 --> 06:41:50,910
discussion as well.

