07:01:24,018 --> 07:01:26,818
if you're going to make a farm, you want to get the water away from it.

4409
07:01:26,998 --> 07:01:30,898
And if you're going to restore a wetland, you want to introduce water back to it.

4410
07:01:31,558 --> 07:01:35,418
So they dammed up the drainage channels, and water came back to the

4411
07:01:35,438 --> 07:01:39,198
Bandon Marsh. It looked like this beautiful brown patch of

4412
07:01:39,238 --> 07:01:39,758
wetland.

4413
07:01:40,598 --> 07:01:44,338
Everything was hunky-dory. The marsh was flooding again, which

4414
07:01:44,418 --> 07:01:47,978
is great for biodiversity. It's especially great for

4415
07:01:48,098 --> 07:01:51,678
specific types of biodiversity. It is great for the salt marsh

4416
07:01:51,758 --> 07:01:52,218
mosquito.

4417
07:01:53,058 --> 07:01:56,698
The people who were restoring this land knew that it would promote

4418
07:01:56,738 --> 07:02:00,358
mosquito habitat. They knew this guy would be coming back in swarms.

4419
07:02:00,538 --> 07:02:04,158
They didn't quite know how much it would become a

4420
07:02:04,198 --> 07:02:05,158
mosquito habitat.

4421
07:02:06,118 --> 07:02:07,958
So there was a public health advisory.

4422
07:02:08,378 --> 07:02:12,158
A local golf course had to close because there were swarms and swarms of

4423
07:02:12,198 --> 07:02:13,818
mosquitoes flooding the golf course.

4424
07:02:14,478 --> 07:02:18,218
The US Fish and Wildlife Service had to sample all of the ponds of standing water

4425
07:02:18,278 --> 07:02:21,838
to figure out how many mosquito larvae there were. There were lots.

4426
07:02:22,298 --> 07:02:24,358
They had to call in excavators again

4427
07:02:25,362 --> 07:02:28,162
To revise the drainage of this area.

4428
07:02:28,282 --> 07:02:30,422
Essentially, they had gotten a little bit too ambitious.

4429
07:02:31,302 --> 07:02:34,362
The tides were flowing in, but they weren't flowing out again.

4430
07:02:35,082 --> 07:02:37,982
So the area was staying wet with standing water the whole time.

4431
07:02:39,422 --> 07:02:43,082
This illustrates a problem in restoration and conservation,

4432
07:02:43,422 --> 07:02:46,082
and that problem is something that others have talked about.

4433
07:02:46,402 --> 07:02:49,762
We work in silos, and so there is a large amount of

4434
07:02:49,802 --> 07:02:52,642
restoration ecology, especially wetland restoration.

4435
07:02:53,442 --> 07:02:57,122
There's a large amount of knowledge on mosquito ecology, but the two do not

4436
07:02:57,162 --> 07:02:59,262
communicate. They are not interoperable.

4437
07:02:59,642 --> 07:03:01,922
These knowledges are locked

4438
07:03:03,262 --> 07:03:06,202
in the literature, and they remain disconnected from one another.

4439
07:03:06,802 --> 07:03:10,682
So the challenge that EcoWeaver has been trying to solve is how do we connect

4440
07:03:10,742 --> 07:03:11,022
this,

4441
07:03:11,982 --> 07:03:15,582
and how do we share it with practitioners, people on the ground, in a way that is

4442
07:03:15,642 --> 07:03:17,122
meaningful and useful to them?

4443
07:03:19,362 --> 07:03:23,162
Part of this quest has been thinking about different ways to approach structured

4444
07:03:23,202 --> 07:03:27,142
data. This way is something that I came up with while I was

4445
07:03:27,202 --> 07:03:31,122
on parental leave with my kid and thought, "Hey, this would be a great

4446
07:03:31,422 --> 07:03:34,942
book to read." It's based on a book by Phyllis Illari and Federico

4447
07:03:35,042 --> 07:03:38,742
Russo called "Causality," and what they propose in this

4448
07:03:39,262 --> 07:03:42,882
is an idea in the philosophy of science that causality is not

4449
07:03:42,962 --> 07:03:45,602
one arrow from one thing to another.

4450
07:03:45,642 --> 07:03:49,502
There are a mosaic of ways of understanding causality, and

4451
07:03:49,562 --> 07:03:52,102
all of those are at play in some way.

4452
07:03:53,042 --> 07:03:56,942
At the same time, I also discovered LinkML, and I started talking a lot more with

4453
07:03:56,982 --> 07:03:59,662
Chris Mungall and the group at the Lawrence Berkeley National Lab.

4454
07:04:01,082 --> 07:04:05,042
And that led to the creation of the Causal Mosaic Schema, which is the icon on

4455
07:04:05,082 --> 07:04:05,922
the far side there.

4456
07:04:07,302 --> 07:04:11,282
And the schema is a labeled property graph schema, which

4457
07:04:11,362 --> 07:04:15,342
I don't think I need to define for this audience, but the idea of a labeled

4458
07:04:15,362 --> 07:04:18,882
property graph is that the nodes and the edges can have

4459
07:04:18,922 --> 07:04:22,562
properties assigned to them. So a relationship

4460
07:04:22,602 --> 07:04:26,242
between ditch plugging process, which is a

4461
07:04:26,302 --> 07:04:30,082
name for the process when you plug the ditches on a farm to restore the wetland,

4462
07:04:31,322 --> 07:04:35,162
that is associated with an increased abundance of the salt marsh

4463
07:04:35,181 --> 07:04:35,681
mosquito.

4464
07:04:37,342 --> 07:04:40,182
And what that looks like in JSON is something like this.

4465
07:04:40,522 --> 07:04:44,322
You can have the entity term grounded in an ontology.

4466
07:04:44,742 --> 07:04:48,642
In this case, it's an ontology of ecological management terms that we

4467
07:04:48,662 --> 07:04:50,542
have also created and been working on.

4468
07:04:51,542 --> 07:04:54,402
So the ditch plugging process is the plain language and then a curie

4469
07:04:55,362 --> 07:04:58,462
associated with it, and the edge can also have

4470
07:04:59,322 --> 07:05:02,922
an ecosystem context. In this case, it was the marsh

4471
07:05:03,062 --> 07:05:03,942
ecosystem type,

4472
07:05:05,422 --> 07:05:07,282
and they can be connected to a node.

4473
07:05:07,362 --> 07:05:09,562
So this is just a brief illustration.

4474
07:05:10,162 --> 07:05:14,122
So if we go back to our mosquito example, we can find in 1995, Kramer

4475
07:05:14,182 --> 07:05:17,322
et al. had a paper that talked about how to

4476
07:05:17,842 --> 07:05:20,602
alleviate the production of

4477
07:05:21,702 --> 07:05:25,642
the salt marsh mosquito in a restored area,

4478
07:05:25,802 --> 07:05:29,302
and that's by tidal enhancement. So you increase the amount of tidal

4479
07:05:29,422 --> 07:05:33,282
turnover, the capacity of the tide to flood and drain from the land.

4480
07:05:35,582 --> 07:05:38,542
What we could do is annotate this claim

4481
07:05:39,362 --> 07:05:43,242
with the Causal Mosaic, and that would lead to a series of nodes and edges.

4482
07:05:44,082 --> 07:05:48,062
Some pretty straightforward stuff, but it gets to be powerful when you expand

4483
07:05:48,082 --> 07:05:51,982
it to thousands of studies. So the restoration of full tidal action

4484
07:05:52,122 --> 07:05:55,982
increases tidal circulation, which is a property of the marsh,

4485
07:05:56,662 --> 07:06:00,562
that could be measured, sometimes we call that hydroperiod,

4486
07:06:01,522 --> 07:06:03,662
which causes a decreased abundance.

4487
07:06:03,762 --> 07:06:07,202
The increasing the circulation causes the larvae to decrease,

4488
07:06:08,242 --> 07:06:08,642
and that

4489
07:06:09,502 --> 07:06:12,842
decreased frequency of herbicide application is something that the Kramer paper was

4490
07:06:12,882 --> 07:06:16,282
concerned with. Not really relevant to our current example, but nonetheless appears

4491
07:06:16,322 --> 07:06:16,762
in the graph.

4492
07:06:18,562 --> 07:06:22,042
So the Causal Mosaic has rich detail on its nodes.

4493
07:06:22,282 --> 07:06:26,082
Like I mentioned before, we have an ontology-grounded entity type, a

4494
07:06:26,142 --> 07:06:29,722
measured attribute which applies to a taxa that might be

4495
07:06:29,762 --> 07:06:33,282
documented here because, as Lars likes to remind me,

4496
07:06:33,762 --> 07:06:37,042
you're not actually talking about the taxa, you're talking about the abundance of

4497
07:06:37,082 --> 07:06:40,382
the taxa or some property of it in this particular area.

4498
07:06:42,322 --> 07:06:45,882
So we concentrate on three main properties

4499
07:06:46,041 --> 07:06:49,302
for the causal nodes: the entity type,

4500
07:06:49,882 --> 07:06:53,382
which could be limited to a taxa, an environmental variable, a

4501
07:06:53,442 --> 07:06:56,202
management process, or an environmental process,

4502
07:06:57,402 --> 07:07:00,982
and then an entity term connected to that, and a state or change qualifier.

4503
07:07:01,062 --> 07:07:03,802
The important thing here is that the nodes are not

4504
07:07:04,622 --> 07:07:08,602
a thing in and of themselves. The nodes describe a change, change

4505
07:07:08,642 --> 07:07:09,282
in something.

4506
07:07:10,642 --> 07:07:14,502
Sometimes that change is everything staying the same, but we can get to that

4507
07:07:14,542 --> 07:07:16,322
later. That's a little bit of complexity in there.

4508
07:07:17,642 --> 07:07:21,522
But the key idea is that causality is a relationship

4509
07:07:21,562 --> 07:07:25,042
between a change in one thing and a change, or a staying the same, in another

4510
07:07:25,082 --> 07:07:25,262
thing.

4511
07:07:27,162 --> 07:07:31,102
The measured attribute, again, describes taxa, and applied to is

4512
07:07:31,142 --> 07:07:33,962
for the target scope of a given process or a variable.

4513
07:07:34,962 --> 07:07:36,822
The edges are much richer.

4514
07:07:37,762 --> 07:07:39,362
We have a

4515
07:07:40,702 --> 07:07:44,682
bunch of details. 11 different causal predicates can be used on an

4516
07:07:44,762 --> 07:07:45,002
edge.

4517
07:07:45,862 --> 07:07:49,842
I think there are 67 fields in total, and

4518
07:07:49,882 --> 07:07:53,862
they describe 16 different causal features and eight philosophical accounts

4519
07:07:53,922 --> 07:07:56,322
in three families. You're not meant to read this.

4520
07:07:56,382 --> 07:07:57,062
It's just meant to

4521
07:07:57,962 --> 07:07:59,622
convey that there's a lot of information.

4522
07:08:00,462 --> 07:08:02,962
That breaks down into four discrete layers.

4523
07:08:03,082 --> 07:08:06,542
So the first layer is claim strength and the linguistic expression, and what this

4524
07:08:06,602 --> 07:08:10,262
is designed for is we are not evaluating the claims that

4525
07:08:10,342 --> 07:08:13,602
authors make, we are transcribing them into the Causal Mosaic.

4526
07:08:13,722 --> 07:08:17,542
So it's not up to this schema to say whether or not the claim is justified.

4527
07:08:18,602 --> 07:08:21,342
It's the claim was made in a peer-reviewed study.

4528
07:08:22,622 --> 07:08:26,362
The second layer is that philosophical account classification, which generally

4529
07:08:26,462 --> 07:08:30,142
breaks down into mechanistic classifications or difference-making

4530
07:08:30,202 --> 07:08:30,602
accounts

4531
07:08:32,050 --> 07:08:35,690
And then the causal features are 16 different features that a given

4532
07:08:35,750 --> 07:08:39,370
causal claim can have. These range from strength to

4533
07:08:39,429 --> 07:08:40,690
context specificity.

4534
07:08:42,630 --> 07:08:46,190
And then the evidential basis is where we talk about what type of study

4535
07:08:46,830 --> 07:08:47,930
led to this finding.

4536
07:08:48,770 --> 07:08:52,450
We also, of course, include full provenance information metadata on the

4537
07:08:52,510 --> 07:08:53,810
article and the annotator.

4538
07:08:55,770 --> 07:08:59,490
I should say too, and I'll talk about this in more detail, because of the scope of

4539
07:08:59,510 --> 07:09:03,450
our project, this necessitates LLM assistance in annotation because there

4540
07:09:03,470 --> 07:09:06,910
are simply too many papers. Right now, we have a graduate student who's working on

4541
07:09:06,930 --> 07:09:10,670
papers. She has gotten through two in the last two months, so this

4542
07:09:10,710 --> 07:09:13,310
is very intense work.

4543
07:09:15,270 --> 07:09:19,170
If we do the mosquito example alone, we end up with a

4544
07:09:19,210 --> 07:09:22,810
hairball of 14 articles, 238 nodes.

4545
07:09:23,530 --> 07:09:27,090
This is small potatoes compared to doing a corpus of thousands of

4546
07:09:27,150 --> 07:09:27,570
articles.

4547
07:09:29,750 --> 07:09:33,330
So it's nice to look at, and we love making hairballs as graph people,

4548
07:09:33,870 --> 07:09:37,570
but how can this be useful? And this is where we turn to the output

4549
07:09:37,690 --> 07:09:40,450
side. I come to this work as a practitioner

4550
07:09:41,330 --> 07:09:44,950
first. My PhD was on community practice of

4551
07:09:44,990 --> 07:09:48,850
restoration, and I'm really more comfortable in the mud than I am in rooms

4552
07:09:49,050 --> 07:09:52,850
like this. So I wanted to make something that would be useful to

4553
07:09:52,890 --> 07:09:55,050
practitioners who are making decisions on the ground.

4554
07:09:55,130 --> 07:09:58,190
So we imagine four outputs from the Causal Mosaic.

4555
07:09:58,790 --> 07:10:00,330
The first is an evidence gap map,

4556
07:10:01,690 --> 07:10:05,330
and this is a typical type of map of the evidence

4557
07:10:05,470 --> 07:10:09,430
that the conservation sector has borrowed from healthcare, where

4558
07:10:09,450 --> 07:10:10,890
it shows the strength of evidence.

4559
07:10:11,110 --> 07:10:14,590
On the x-axis could be a particular

4560
07:10:14,730 --> 07:10:15,210
technique

4561
07:10:16,190 --> 07:10:19,510
or a particular aspect of the ecosystem, and on the y-axis could be

4562
07:10:20,950 --> 07:10:21,470
another thing.

4563
07:10:22,990 --> 07:10:26,870
A better example is you could have the restoration technique along the x-axis and

4564
07:10:26,910 --> 07:10:29,790
then the ecosystem where it's applied along the y, and it would show you the

4565
07:10:29,830 --> 07:10:32,210
strength of evidence and illustrate where the gaps might be.

4566
07:10:33,570 --> 07:10:36,730
The second is a more direct decision support tool. Thanks.

4567
07:10:37,390 --> 07:10:41,210
This is a fuzzy cognitive map, and this was first proposed by

4568
07:10:41,250 --> 07:10:43,850
Bart Kosko way back in the '70s.

4569
07:10:44,770 --> 07:10:48,670
And it has been extended and expanded since, but it is a very basic

4570
07:10:48,690 --> 07:10:49,730
decision-making model,

4571
07:10:50,630 --> 07:10:54,210
where the relative proportion of the edges are what's important, not

4572
07:10:54,250 --> 07:10:56,290
necessarily the values themselves.

4573
07:10:56,750 --> 07:11:00,630
But the Causal Mosaic can translate fairly directly into a

4574
07:11:00,690 --> 07:11:04,170
map like this, so you could specify what different components of your

4575
07:11:04,230 --> 07:11:08,170
ecosystem you're interested in and create a fuzzy cognitive map, and then you could

4576
07:11:08,310 --> 07:11:11,810
run some tests to see what happens when you change certain

4577
07:11:11,910 --> 07:11:14,170
parameters, what happens to other parameters.

4578
07:11:15,290 --> 07:11:16,670
We can get a little bit more detailed

4579
07:11:17,510 --> 07:11:21,490
and create a Bayesian belief network out of parts of the Causal Mosaic.

4580
07:11:22,110 --> 07:11:25,770
And for this, the Causal Mosaic is helpful in forming the metal model for the

4581
07:11:25,790 --> 07:11:29,750
Bayesian belief network. So that means the structure, what the different

4582
07:11:31,090 --> 07:11:34,730
parameters are, and also for providing some information for the

4583
07:11:34,770 --> 07:11:38,250
priors in that network. So some idea of the range of

4584
07:11:38,290 --> 07:11:42,150
variation that's been documented in the studies that could help you

4585
07:11:42,190 --> 07:11:44,650
inform priors. It's not a direct one-to-one.

4586
07:11:44,730 --> 07:11:47,430
You couldn't programmatically make a Bayesian belief network.

4587
07:11:47,470 --> 07:11:49,370
It does require some intervention, but

4588
07:11:50,270 --> 07:11:52,050
it nonetheless gets you halfway there.

4589
07:11:53,130 --> 07:11:55,230
The fourth output that we envision

4590
07:11:56,190 --> 07:11:58,730
is a retrieval-augmented generation type

4591
07:11:59,570 --> 07:12:03,510
approach, where you pair an LLM with the hairball, and

4592
07:12:03,550 --> 07:12:07,310
the LLM has the capability to construct searches and

4593
07:12:07,350 --> 07:12:11,150
provide a meaningful output to a practitioner who asks a

4594
07:12:11,210 --> 07:12:12,350
natural language query.

4595
07:12:14,090 --> 07:12:16,910
Lots of limitations because it's a big, ambitious project.

4596
07:12:17,290 --> 07:12:20,530
So this is a meta-model, not the parameters.

4597
07:12:20,610 --> 07:12:23,310
We aren't really pulling in all of that granular data.

4598
07:12:23,730 --> 07:12:25,950
You'll notice we're not talking about effect size.

4599
07:12:26,830 --> 07:12:29,010
We're not talking about P values. We're not talking

4600
07:12:29,870 --> 07:12:32,750
about the statistical tests that these papers contain.

4601
07:12:32,850 --> 07:12:36,510
So that means it can't really provide a fully parameterized

4602
07:12:36,590 --> 07:12:39,330
model. That's a direction we hope to grow into.

4603
07:12:41,110 --> 07:12:44,470
This focuses on the Western academic science tradition,

4604
07:12:45,270 --> 07:12:49,010
especially in discussing what experiment types led to a particular causal

4605
07:12:49,110 --> 07:12:49,349
link.

4606
07:12:50,450 --> 07:12:52,349
So that's a big gap. We are working on that.

4607
07:12:52,510 --> 07:12:56,310
We're having a gathering in Jena, Germany next week, where we have a lot of

4608
07:12:56,350 --> 07:12:59,830
people coming to talk about traditional ecological knowledge and how that might

4609
07:12:59,890 --> 07:13:01,170
factor into causality.

4610
07:13:02,930 --> 07:13:05,450
And importantly, it focuses on peer-reviewed literature.

4611
07:13:05,530 --> 07:13:08,950
Within conservation and restoration, there's a lot of information contained in

4612
07:13:08,990 --> 07:13:12,430
management reports, which often don't use the same kind of experimental

4613
07:13:12,590 --> 07:13:14,870
structure that we see in the literature.

4614
07:13:15,630 --> 07:13:19,210
So we haven't included those yet, and part of the reason is that the

4615
07:13:19,270 --> 07:13:23,110
literature is comparatively easy to search and easy to

4616
07:13:23,150 --> 07:13:25,510
download depending on how you do it.

4617
07:13:26,230 --> 07:13:30,090
So it's easier for us to pilot this model,

4618
07:13:30,430 --> 07:13:34,150
change it in ways that might make it a little better, more effective.

4619
07:13:34,670 --> 07:13:37,890
And the final one is that this relies on LLM annotation.

4620
07:13:38,910 --> 07:13:42,130
I could do a whole separate presentation on that pipeline and the question of

